{
  "$comment": [
    "MEASURED non-apt wall time for every job `audit-apt-budget-fits-job-timeout.ts` governs.",
    "",
    "WHAT A ROW IS. `nonAptP90Seconds` is the p90, over `samples` recent SUCCESSFUL runs, of",
    "(job wall seconds - the longest single apt phase in that run). It is everything that job does",
    "OTHER than the apt phase the budget bounds: checkout, cache restore, the rest of install.sh,",
    "the build, the tests, and every `Post` step. The audit adds it to the apt budget and the",
    "SIGKILL grace and requires the sum to fit inside `timeout-minutes`.",
    "",
    "WHY IT IS MEASURED AND NOT ASSUMED. The audit used to carry ONE constant, 120s, described in",
    "its own comment as 'the work that PRECEDES it' -- with no term at all for the work that",
    "follows. The rows below are why one number could never have worked: the same term is 71s for",
    "accelerator-local-llm-validate and 795s for k8s-argocd-health-test:live-kind-included. Any",
    "single value would have been a guess for 44 jobs.",
    "",
    "WHY THE LONGEST PHASE AND NOT THE SUM. The budget bounds ONE invocation of the apt phase. The",
    "docker legs run install.sh three times per job; the worst case the audit must survive is one of",
    "those spending its entire budget while the others cost what they normally cost.",
    "",
    "PROVENANCE, so the number is checkable rather than asserted: `runIds` lists the exact runs and",
    "`window` their date range. Re-derive with",
    "`bun src/Core.TypeScript/hygiene/refresh-apt-job-timings.ts` (needs `gh`).",
    "",
    "HONEST LIMIT. This is a committed SNAPSHOT. A job that grows slower after it was measured is",
    "under-charged until someone refreshes it. The alternative -- calling the Actions API from the",
    "audit itself -- would make the check pass whenever the network is down, which is the exact",
    "failure mode ('a check that did not run looking like one that passed') the audit exists to",
    "stop. Small `samples` counts are load-bearing information, not noise: chart-version-refresh",
    "has n=1 because the workflow rarely runs, and its p90 is therefore a single observation.",
    "",
    "`unmeasured` NAMES THE JOBS NO RUN COULD MEASURE, individually, with the reason. They are not",
    "skipped: the audit falls back to the old, weaker PRE_APT_RESERVE_SECONDS bound for them and",
    "says so in its output. A governed job that appears in NEITHER list fails the audit outright."
  ],
